    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d386">

                <pb facs="0427" n="415" sameAs="frus:frus1943v05/pg415"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">835.00/1363: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Armour</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos
                                Aires</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-02-18T11:00:00-03:00">February 18, 1943—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 1:07 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>384. Military Attaché<anchor n="82" xml:id="d386-f1"/> informed yesterday by
                    friendly high ranking Argentine army officer that Argentine General Staff
                    recently submitted memorandum to Minister of War<anchor n="83" xml:id="d386-f2"
                    /> expressing opinion that Argentina’s neutrality policy wrong and dangerous
                    because neighboring countries increasing armament through Lend-Lease<anchor
                        n="84" xml:id="d386-f3"/> and Argentina’s military establishment not strong
                    enough to resist aggression therefore recommending change of policy to accord
                    with Rio<anchor n="85" xml:id="d386-f4"/> and other commitments. Minister of War
                    transmitted memo to President<anchor n="86" xml:id="d386-f5"/> whose answer was
                    “the policy will not be changed. If the army is not strong reinforce it.”
                    Regional commanders have accordingly been ordered to prepare and submit by March
                    1st studies for raising 30,000 additional men to reinforce the 40,000 normally
                    conscripted. Informant stated there will not be suitable shelter, clothing,
                    arms, etc., for these additional conscripts.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Armour</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d386-f1" n="82" xml:id="d386-f1-content">
                <p>Col. John W. Lang.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d386-f2" n="83" xml:id="d386-f2-content">
                <p>Gen. Pedro P. Ramírez.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d386-f3" n="84" xml:id="d386-f3-content">
                <p>For correspondence on the Argentine Lend-Lease discussions, see <ref
                        target="frus1941v06#pg_323"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>,
                        1941, <hi rendition="#small-caps">vi</hi>, pp. 323</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d386-f4" n="85" xml:id="d386-f4-content">
                <p>For correspondence on the Third Meeting of the Foreign Ministers of the American
                    Republics, held at Rio de Janeiro, January 15–28, 1942, see <ref
                        target="frus1942v05#pg_6"><hi rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, 1942, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">v</hi>, pp. 6</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d386-f5" n="86" xml:id="d386-f5-content">
                <p>Ramón Castillo.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
